# Proteus Testing Conventions

## Test markers

Every test is automatically labeled by `tests/conftest.py` based on its directory and xfail target. Do not add markers manually unless adding a new category.

Registered markers (defined in `code/proteus/pytest.ini`):

| Marker | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `foundation` | Unit tests for low-level primitives (moments, ANN, nodes, rates, links, deferred, Oja, splits, pruning, stabilization, intrinsic dim) |
| `property` | SI-formula invariant tests |
| `reduction` | Theorem-reduction numerical checks |
| `stage1` | Stage 1 scaffold, scale, and controller tests |
| `stage2` | Stage 2 complex, torsion, dual-flow, and density tests |
| `evidence` | Evidence gate, DM score, and star-matrix tests |
| `inference` | Membership, sampling, and query-interface tests |
| `diagnostics` | Junction detector, expressivity ledger, and boundary tests |
| `scenario` | End-to-end scenario tests (synthetic or real) |
| `synthetic` | Synthetic dataset scenarios |
| `real_data` | Real-data scenarios requiring cached external datasets |
| `benchmark` | Performance and memory benchmark tests |
| `awaiting` | Test is expected to xfail until a named implementation module lands |

## xfail convention

Tests awaiting unimplemented modules use the `@awaiting` decorator from `tests.harness.markers`:

```python
from tests.harness.markers import awaiting

@awaiting("stage1.controller", si="S2.5")
def test_circle_scale_recovery():
    pytest.fail("Not implemented")
```

When the module lands, remove the decorator and replace the body with real assertions.

## Marker summary

Every `pipenv run pytest` run ends with a marker-grouped summary table showing passed/xfailed/failed counts per marker. This is produced by `pytest_terminal_summary` in `tests/conftest.py`.

## Running subsets

```bash
pipenv run pytest -m foundation        # only foundation tests
pipenv run pytest -m "stage1 and not awaiting"  # only passing stage1 tests
pipenv run pytest -m "not real_data"    # skip real-data tests (default)
pipenv run pytest -m benchmark          # only benchmarks
```

## Reporting to user

When reporting test results, always include:
1. Total passed / xfailed / failed counts.
2. The marker summary table from the end of the run.
3. For any failures, the full test name, marker, and xfail reason if applicable.
